WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.247
RUFUS: Does the distinction matter
if the output is

00:00:01.247 --> 00:00:02.494
RUFUS: the same?

00:00:02.579 --> 00:00:05.188
ALEX: That's actually a good
question. I think it

00:00:05.188 --> 00:00:07.797
ALEX: matters for what you do
about it. If

00:00:07.797 --> 00:00:10.406
ALEX: a colleague is upset, you
manage the relationship.

00:00:10.406 --> 00:00:13.015
ALEX: If a model is producing
worse outputs because

00:00:13.015 --> 00:00:15.624
ALEX: of your phrasing, you change
the phrasing. Same

00:00:15.624 --> 00:00:18.233
ALEX: fix, different reason.

00:00:18.343 --> 00:00:21.419
JAMIE: Right. And the practical
implication is really simple:

00:00:21.419 --> 00:00:24.495
JAMIE: polite, specific, clear
prompts perform better than curt,

00:00:24.495 --> 00:00:27.571
JAMIE: vague, or hostile ones. Not
because the AI

00:00:27.571 --> 00:00:30.647
JAMIE: cares about your tone
emotionally. But because the

00:00:30.647 --> 00:00:33.723
JAMIE: structure of a polite,
specific prompt is usually

00:00:33.723 --> 00:00:36.799
JAMIE: also a better-structured
prompt.
